    <briefdescription>
<para>Slab-based VRAM memory pool. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Pre-divides a fixed VRAM budget into equal-sized slabs and serves allocation requests from the pool. When no slab is large enough to satisfy a request the caller receives a &quot;pool miss&quot; (returns false) so it can fall back to the on-demand allocator or CPU path.</para>
<sect1 id="classthemis_1_1gpu_1_1_g_p_u_memory_pool_1autotoc_md32_1s1"><sect2 id="classthemis_1_1gpu_1_1_g_p_u_memory_pool_1autotoc_md32">
<title>Design notes</title><para><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>Slabs are logical records only (no real cudaMalloc). The pool tracks VRAM budget at the bookkeeping level; real device allocation is gated behind THEMIS_ENABLE_CUDA / THEMIS_ENABLE_HIP.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>All methods are protected by a single internal mutex; this is intentionally simple — a production implementation would use lock-free free-lists per size class.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Fragmentation is tracked as the fraction of wasted bytes across all live allocations.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para>Thread safety: all public methods are protected by an internal mutex. </para>
</sect2></sect1>
    </detaileddescription>
